WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:05.171
Tally up all the Grand Slam championships

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:07.807
won by current WTA players,

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:09.275
and you'll notice that nearly half

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:11.678
belong to just one person:

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:13.213
Serena Williams.

00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:17.550
In fact, Serena is one
of the most successful

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.152
tennis players of all time.

00:00:19.152 --> 00:00:23.490
And that's in large part
thanks to her iconic serve.

00:00:31.331 --> 00:00:33.666
You can break Serena's
serve into four parts.

00:00:34.701 --> 00:00:37.437
The acceptance window
is the area of the court

00:00:37.437 --> 00:00:40.640
where the ball has to land in
order for the serve to count.

00:00:41.474 --> 00:00:43.676
It's determined by the minimum angle

00:00:43.676 --> 00:00:45.412
for the ball to clear the net

00:00:45.412 --> 00:00:49.115
and the maximum angle
for it to land inbounds.

00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:50.717
So, you can see here that the height

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:54.521
at which you hit the ball
makes a big difference.

00:00:54.521 --> 00:00:56.122
Chang Kee Jung: As
taller you are, you have

00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:57.857
more acceptance window.

00:00:57.857 --> 00:01:01.728
Imagine Yao Ming learns to play tennis,

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:03.329
and if he hits the serve,

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:05.532
he will have a huge acceptance window.

00:01:05.532 --> 00:01:08.168
Narrator: Serena isn't
nearly as tall as Yao,

00:01:08.168 --> 00:01:09.769
and she's not even among the tallest

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:12.639
female tennis players out there.

00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:14.474
But she makes up for it.

00:01:14.474 --> 00:01:17.777
Chang: So, Serena Williams
does exceptionally well.

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:19.279
At the position when she hits,

00:01:19.279 --> 00:01:21.214
her arms are fully stretched

00:01:21.214 --> 00:01:23.516
and then she has a little bit of jump.

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:25.852
Narrator: The stretch
and jump boost the height

00:01:25.852 --> 00:01:27.253
at which she hits the ball,

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:29.122
increasing her acceptance window

00:01:29.122 --> 00:01:31.424
and ultimately improving her chances

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:33.560
of hitting it inbounds.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.063
Now, Serena isn't the only
player who extends on the serve.

00:01:37.063 --> 00:01:40.567
But, according to experts,
she is much more consistent.

00:01:40.567 --> 00:01:42.869
And it pays off.

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:44.070
Over the last 10 years,

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:47.674
she's won 82% of the
games that she served.

00:01:47.674 --> 00:01:50.243
That's a whopping 11% higher

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:53.079
than the average of the
top 20 female players

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:54.981
over the same time frame.

00:01:54.981 --> 00:01:58.284
But, of course, serving the ball inbounds

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:00.320
is just the first step.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:02.388
What gets her all of those wins

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:04.023
is the fact that she makes her serves

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:06.226
extremely difficult to return.

00:02:06.226 --> 00:02:08.328
That's where power and topspin come in.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:13.566
Serena's power serve is one
of the fastest in the game,

00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:16.636
averaging 170 kilometers per hour.

00:02:16.636 --> 00:02:19.239
That's about 12 kilometers per hour faster

00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:21.941
than the average female
professional tennis player.

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:26.412
Now, part of that speed is
the result of pure strength.

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:27.814
Chang: Serena Williams, you can just see

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:31.384
she has, like, incredible,
lean, muscular body,

00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:34.220
so she can generate a
huge amount of force.

00:02:35.889 --> 00:02:39.225
Narrator: But what really sets
Serena's power serve apart

00:02:39.225 --> 00:02:40.260
is her timing.

00:02:41.094 --> 00:02:43.163
To maximize power on the serve,

00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:45.365
tennis players will bend their knees,

00:02:45.365 --> 00:02:47.333
rotate and drop the hip,

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:48.968
lower the shoulder,

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:51.504
and, most importantly, twist at the trunk.

00:02:52.906 --> 00:02:55.708
In other words, they use their whole body

00:02:55.708 --> 00:02:58.444
to gain as much velocity
behind the serve as possible.

00:02:59.479 --> 00:03:01.614
But none of that matters
if you don't hit the ball

00:03:01.614 --> 00:03:04.651
at the exact moment when
your arm is at full reach

00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:06.085
and your body's in mid-swing.

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:09.989
In tennis, this is called fluidity.

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:12.025
And Serena is often credited

00:03:12.025 --> 00:03:14.227
with the most fluid serve in the sport.

00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:17.397
Chang: There will be no jerky motions.

00:03:17.397 --> 00:03:20.300
You will smoothly hit the
ball with maximum power.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:22.635
Narrator: But there's a
catch to these power serves.

00:03:25.205 --> 00:03:27.340
Yes, they're fast, but the velocity

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:29.175
narrows the acceptance window,

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:30.910
making them less reliable.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:34.380
That's why Serena also uses
topspin to her advantage,

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.517
usually when her first power
serve goes out of bounds.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:42.555
Now, topspin serves are slower.

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:45.592
For example, Serena's
average topspin serve

00:03:45.592 --> 00:03:49.429
is about 145 kilometers per hour,

00:03:49.429 --> 00:03:53.633
25 kilometers per hour
slower than her power serve.

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:55.969
But topspin serves are more accurate

00:03:55.969 --> 00:03:58.238
and still difficult to return.

00:03:58.238 --> 00:03:59.939
That's because the topspin on the ball

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:01.908
forces it to drop sharply

00:04:01.908 --> 00:04:04.477
right before it reaches the opponent.

00:04:04.477 --> 00:04:07.180
That change in movement
is thanks to a phenomenon

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:08.748
called the Magnus effect.

00:04:09.582 --> 00:04:11.117
As the ball spins through the air,

00:04:11.117 --> 00:04:13.987
it creates a pocket of lower
pressure underneath it.

00:04:13.987 --> 00:04:15.455
And it's that low pressure

00:04:15.455 --> 00:04:17.757
that makes the ball drop abnormally fast,

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:20.360
catching many opponents off guard.

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:23.896
But Serena's second serve is
not the fastest in the game.

00:04:23.896 --> 00:04:25.798
Maria Sharapova, for example,

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:30.203
will fire her second serve at
about 150 kilometers per hour.

00:04:30.203 --> 00:04:33.806
But Sharapova sacrifices
accuracy for speed here.

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:37.076
For comparison, Serena
has scored, on average,

00:04:37.076 --> 00:04:39.579
2 1/2 fewer double faults per match

00:04:39.579 --> 00:04:42.448
than Sharapova over the last 10 years.

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:44.817
Chang: So, she combines the first serve,

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:48.221
of high speed, which is
a little bit more risky,

00:04:48.221 --> 00:04:50.790
but her second serve is safer.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:54.027
However, it has a high topspin,
and it's awkward to return.

00:04:54.027 --> 00:04:57.130
And combining these two,
she becomes excellent,

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:58.898
excellent service player.

00:04:58.898 --> 00:05:01.934
Narrator: Meaning she
scores more aces on average.

00:05:02.869 --> 00:05:04.604
An ace is a successful serve

00:05:04.604 --> 00:05:06.706
that the opponent's racket cannot reach

00:05:06.706 --> 00:05:08.641
and therefore doesn't return.

00:05:08.641 --> 00:05:09.909
Over the last 10 years,

00:05:09.909 --> 00:05:12.945
Serena has recorded
four more aces per match

00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:15.715
than the average top 20 WTA players.

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:17.917
Last, but not least,

00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:20.887
is the coup de grâce of Serena's serve:

00:05:20.887 --> 00:05:22.488
disguise.

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:24.624
Chang: Another exceptional quality

00:05:24.624 --> 00:05:27.827
is her ability to serve the ball

00:05:27.827 --> 00:05:29.329
without telling the opponent

00:05:29.329 --> 00:05:31.531
what kind of serve she will make.

00:05:31.531 --> 00:05:32.865
Narrator: Check out this ball toss.

00:05:32.865 --> 00:05:34.967
Normally, the way a player tosses the ball

00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:38.538
will give their opponent a clue
as to what serve is coming.

00:05:38.538 --> 00:05:40.139
But not Serena.

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:42.508
She tosses the ball to the same spot,

00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:44.277
with the same body position,

00:05:44.277 --> 00:05:46.879
every single time.

00:05:46.879 --> 00:05:49.282
Her toss is so consistent and smooth

00:05:49.282 --> 00:05:51.050
that she manages something most others

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:53.252
find impossible to replicate.

00:05:53.252 --> 00:05:56.022
She can disguise the serve.

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:58.891
So, Serena could be about
to deliver a topspin serve,

00:05:58.891 --> 00:06:01.828
a power serve, or even a slice serve.

00:06:01.828 --> 00:06:04.931
Yet the opponent has no way
of knowing what's coming.

00:06:05.932 --> 00:06:07.767
Chang: These things are all disguised

00:06:07.767 --> 00:06:09.802
until the very last moment.

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:11.804
And that makes Serena Williams'

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:15.141
serve exceptionally difficult to return.

00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:19.312
So, for as long as Serena is competing,

00:06:19.312 --> 00:06:22.348
these three words have
the power to strike fear

00:06:22.348 --> 00:06:25.351
into many pro players' hearts:

